Following proposals in TW, we also account for inter-speaker variation by proposing that, even in a single context such as a sociolinguistic interview, different speakers can have different types, i.e. different personae that they are trying to perform. Given this, we would of course like to know how and why different speakers come to have different ideas about which personae will be most useful to them in achieving their goals; however, such an explanation lies at the level of social theory30, not linguistic theory, and therefore is out of the scope of this work. Once the values and types for different speakers are given, the account of social stratification in SMGs is straightforward.
